```python\\ndef select(\\n    self,\\n    filter_func: Callable[[Agent], bool] | None = None,\\n    at_most: int | float | None = None,\\n    inplace: bool = False,\\n    agent_types: type[Agent] | list[type[Agent]] | None = None,\\n    n: int | None = None,\\n) -> AgentSet:\\n```\\n\\nHmm wait, but this testbed has `agent_type` (singular). The user request says \\\"it should work seamlessly with the existing filtering by agent type\\\" — so keep `agent_type` as is. The user didn't ask for `agent_types` (plural/list). Let me not change that.\\n\\nThe user's requirements:\\n1. New parameter `at_most`: int or float 0.0-1.0. Integer → fixed number. Float → fraction, rounded down.\\n2. `n` deprecated with warning; auto-convert n to at_most.\\n3. Upper limit semantics — combined with filters, may return fewer.\\n4. Return agents in current order.\\n5. Chaining with sort, filter, do, set etc.\\n6. No limit → all matching agents.\\n7. inplace behavior as before.\\n\\nImplementation:\\n\\n```python\\ndef select(\\n    self,\\n    filter_func: Callable[[Agent], bool] | None = None,\\n    n: int | None = None,\\n    inplace: bool = False,\\n    agent_type: type[Agent] | None = None,\\n    at_most: int | float | None = None,\\n) -> AgentSet:\\n```\\n\\nHmm, where should `at_most` go in the signature? If added at the end, positional callers with (filter_func, n, inplace, agent_type) keep working. Adding it after agent_type preserves backward compat for positional args. In real Mesa 3.0, they moved `at_most` to position 2 and made `n` keyword-only at the end... Actually in real Mesa the signature is:\\n\\n```python\\ndef select(\\n    self,\\n    filter_func=None,\\n    at_most=None,\\n    inplace=False,\\n    agent_types=None,\\n    n=None,\\n):\\n```\\n\\nBut in this testbed, `n` is currently positional position 2. If someone calls `agentset.select(func, 5)` positionally, with the real Mesa signature that 5 would bind to `at_most` — actually that's the desired migration! In real Mesa, `n` was moved to last position and deprecated.\\n\\nHmm, but the safest approach for backward compatibility given the user's explicit statement \\\"I still want to be able to use my old code that uses the 'n' parameter\\\" — old code might call `select(func, 5)` positionally. If I reorder, `select(func, 5)` would set at_most=5 which is the same behavior! Because n=5 means at most 5. So reordering `at_most` to position 2 and `n` to the end is actually behavior-preserving for positional calls (with deprecation... wait no, positional call `select(func, 5)` would now set at_most=5 without a deprecation warning — which is fine, that's the new parameter).\\n\\nHmm wait, but `select(func, 0)`? Old: n=0 → all. New: at_most=0 → hmm, 0 means... In my implementation, at_most=0 should mean \\\"no limit\\\"? Let's check: with `at_most: int | float | None = None`, None means no limit. What should 0 mean? If 0 means select 0 agents... that would be useless. In real Mesa, let me recall how they handled it.\\n\\nLooking at actual Mesa code (mesa/agent.py in v3.0):\\n\\n```python\\n        if filter_func is None and agent_types is None and at_most is None:\\n            return self if inplace else copy.copy(self)\\n\\n        if n is not None:\\n            warnings.warn(\\n                \\\"The 'n' parameter is deprecated. Use 'at_most' instead.\\\",\\n                DeprecationWarning,\\n                stacklevel=2,\\n            )\\n            at_most = n\\n\\n        # checks if at_most is a float and if so, converts it to an int\\n        if isinstance(at_most, float):\\n            if not 0 < at_most <= 1:\\n                raise ValueError(\\n                    \\\"If at_most is a float, it must be between 0 and 1.\\\"\\n                )\\n            at_most = int(at_most * len(self))\\n```\\n\\nHmm, actually I don't remember exactly. Let me think about what makes sense and matches the user's spec:\\n\\n- \\\"at_most should accept either an integer (to select 