 \n\nHmm, but there's a subtlety — the docstring said \"If 0, all matching agents are selected. Defaults to 0.\" Changing default to None changes introspection but not behavior. I think it's fine and cleaner. Actually, let me think about what the \"reference implementation\" for this testbed might be. This looks like it's based on Mesa PR that added `at_most` to `AgentSet.select`. Let me recall the actual Mesa implementation.\n\nIn Mesa (actual repo), the `select` method was updated in PR #2265 or similar (\"Add at_most parameter to AgentSet.select\"). The actual Mesa 3.0 implementation:\n\n```python\ndef select(\n    self,\n    filter_func: Callable[[Agent], bool] | None = None,\n    at_most: int | float | None = None,\n    inplace: bool = False,\n    agent_types: type[Agent] | list[type[Agent]] | None = None,\n    n: int | None = None,\n) -> AgentSet:\n    \"\"\"Select a subset of agents from the AgentSet based on a filter function and/or quantity limit.\n\n    Args:\n        filter_func (Callable[[Agent], bool], optional): A function that takes an Agent and returns True if the\n            agent should be included in the result. Defaults to None, meaning no filtering is applied.\n        at_most (int | float | None, optional): The maximum amount of agents to select. If None, all matching agents are selected.\n            If a float between 0 and 1, it specifies the fraction of the agents to select (rounded down).\n            If an integer, it specifies the maximum number of agents to select. Default is None.\n        inplace (bool, optional): If True, modifies the current AgentSet; otherwise, returns a new AgentSet. Default is False.\n        agent_types (type[Agent] | list[type[Agent]], optional): The class type(s) of the agents to select.\n        n (int, optional): Deprecated. Use at_most instead.\n\n    Returns:\n        AgentSet: A new AgentSet containing the selected agents, unless inplace is True, in which case the current AgentSet is updated.\n    \"\"\"