es:\\n            The selected agents are returned in the order they appear in the AgentSet.\\n            If you want to select agents randomly, shuffle the AgentSet first.\\n        \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n        if n is not None:\\n            warnings.warn(\\n                \\\"The 'n' parameter is deprecated. Use 'at_most' instead.\\\",\\n                DeprecationWarning,\\n                stacklevel=2,\\n            )\\n            at_most = n\\n\\n        if filter_func is None and agent_types is None and at_most is None:\\n            return self if inplace else copy.copy(self)\\n\\n        def agent_generator(filter_func=None, agent_types=None, at_most=None):\\n            count = 0\\n            for agent in self:\\n                if (not filter_func or filter_func(agent)) and (\\n                    not agent_types or isinstance(agent, agent_types)\\n                ):\\n                    yield agent\\n                    count += 1\\n                    if at_most is not None and count >= at_most:\\n                        break\\n```\\n\\nHmm, wait. That generator logic with `count >= at_most` after yield would break after yielding at_most agents... Actually there's a subtle issue: if the loop breaks after yield when count >= at_most, then for at_most=5, agents yielded: 1..5, after 5th yield count=5 >= 5 → break. So exactly 5. That works.\\n\\nBut actually the original code in this testbed has:\\n\\n```python\\n        def agent_generator(filter_func=None, agent_type=None, n=0):\\n            count = 0\\n            for agent in self:\\n                if (not filter_func or filter_func(agent)) and (\\n                    not agent_type or isinstance(agent, agent_type)\\n                ):\\n                    yield agent\\n                    count += 1\\n                    if 0 < n <= count:\\n                        break\\n```\\n\\nNote: `if 0 < n <= count: break` — this breaks when count >= n > 0. So n=0 → no limit. Same logic.\\n\\nSo the minimal 